  "response_complete": true,
  "response_text": "Feeling isolated in a marriage can be a challenging experience, but there are several de-escalation steps you can take to help manage the situation:\n\n1. **Communicate Openly**: Have an honest conversation with your spouse about your feelings. Express your concerns and listen actively to their perspective. This can help clarify misunderstandings and build trust.\n\n2. **Set Boundaries**: Clearly define what you are and are not willing to tolerate in your relationship. Boundaries can help prevent misunderstandings and conflicts.\n\n3. **Seek Support**: Talk to friends, family, or a counselor who can provide support and help you navigate the situation. Sometimes, external perspectives can offer new insights and solutions.\n\n4. **Practice Self-Care**: Engage in activities that make you feel good and help you relax. This could be anything from exercise, meditation, or hobbies.\n\n5. **Focus on the Present**: Try to stay present in the moment and avoid dwelling on past issues. This can help reduce stress and improve your ability to communicate effectively.\n\n6. **Take Breaks**: If you feel overwhelmed, take breaks from the situation. This could mean taking a walk, doing something you enjoy, or even just stepping away for a few minutes.\n\n7. **Consider Professional Help**: If the isolation persists and you feel unable to manage on your own, consider seeking help from a marriage counselor or therapist.\n\nRemember, it's important to take care of yourself during this time. Isolation can be harmful, and taking steps to address it can help improve your overall well-being and the health of your marriage.",
